## Iterator Pattern – What & Why?

The **Iterator** pattern gives a standard way to traverse a collection of objects **without exposing the underlying representation** (array, linked list, database result set, etc.).  
It separates the “how” the collection is stored from “how” it is walked.

| Role | Responsibility | Typical Java Type |
|------|----------------|-------------------|
| **Iterator** | Exposes traversal operations (`hasNext()`, `next()`, optionally `remove()`) | `java.util.Iterator<E>` |
| **ConcreteIterator** | Implements traversal logic for a specific collection | `ListIterator`, `ArrayListIterator`, etc. |
| **Aggregate / Collection** | Holds the data & creates an iterator | `java.util.Collection<E>`, `Iterable<E>` |
| **ConcreteAggregate** | Implements the collection’s data storage & iterator creation | `ArrayList<E>`, `LinkedList<E>`, custom `ShoppingCart` |

The pattern is one of the “Gang of Four” design patterns and is used everywhere in Java (think `for‑each` loops, streams, XML parsers, etc.).

---

## How It Works (High‑Level Flow)

```
Aggregate      Iterator
   |                |
   | createIterator()  |
   |----------------->|  <-- returns an Iterator
   |                |
   |                |  while (it.hasNext())
   |                |     element = it.next()
   |                |     // use element
   |                |
```

The client never needs to know how the iterator walks the underlying structure.

---

## A Simple Java Example

Let’s implement a tiny collection of `Item` objects and provide a custom iterator.  
(Afterward we’ll see how Java’s standard library does the same thing.)

### 1. Domain Objects

```java
public class Item {
    private final String name;
    private final double price;

    public Item(String name, double price) {
        this.name  = name;
        this.price = price;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + " ($" + price + ")";
    }
}
```

### 2. The Collection (Aggregate)

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class ShoppingCart {
    private final List<Item> items = new ArrayList<>();

    // Simple mutation API
    public void add(Item i) { items.add(i); }
    public void remove(Item i) { items.remove(i); }

    // Create a new iterator for this cart
    public ShoppingCartIterator iterator() {
        return new ShoppingCartIterator(this);
    }

    // Give access to the underlying list (used only by the iterator)
    List<Item> getItems() { return items; }
}
```

> **Why not just expose the list?**  
> Exposing the internal `List<Item>` would break encapsulation.  
> The iterator hides that detail and guarantees a stable interface.

### 3. The Iterator (ConcreteIterator)

```java
public class ShoppingCartIterator implements java.util.Iterator<Item> {
    private final ShoppingCart cart;
    private int currentIndex = 0;   // next element to return

    public ShoppingCartIterator(ShoppingCart cart) {
        this.cart = cart;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean hasNext() {
        return currentIndex < cart.getItems().size();
    }

    @Override
    public Item next() {
        return cart.getItems().get(currentIndex++);
    }

    @Override
    public void remove() {
        if (currentIndex == 0) throw new IllegalStateException();
        cart.getItems().remove(--currentIndex);
    }
}
```

### 4. Client Code

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ShoppingCart cart = new ShoppingCart();
        cart.add(new Item("Laptop", 1200.00));
        cart.add(new Item("Headphones", 150.00));
        cart.add(new Item("Coffee Mug", 12.99));

        // Iterate over the cart
        ShoppingCartIterator it = cart.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            Item item = it.next();
            System.out.println(item);
        }

        // Removing while iterating
        it = cart.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            Item item = it.next();
            if (item.toString().contains("Headphones")) {
                it.remove();          // safely removes it from cart
            }
        }
        System.out.println("After removal: " + cart.iterator().hasNext()); // false if only head
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
Laptop ($1200.0)
Headphones ($150.0)
Coffee Mug ($12.99)
After removal: false   // depends on remaining items
```

---

## Using Java’s Built‑In Iterator

Java’s standard collections already implement the Iterator pattern.

```java
List<Item> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add(new Item("Laptop", 1200));
list.add(new Item("Phone", 800));

for (Item i : list) {               // internally uses Iterator
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

Under the hood the compiler rewrites the enhanced `for` loop into:

```java
Iterator<Item> it = list.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    Item i = it.next();
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

> **Key takeaway:**  
> You don’t need to write your own iterator if you use `java.util.Collection` or `Iterable`.  
> Implementing your own iterator is useful only when you have a *custom* data structure that does not already provide one.

---

## Summary

| Point | What it means |
|-------|---------------|
| **Goal** | Traverse a collection without exposing its internals. |
| **Components** | Iterator, ConcreteIterator, Aggregate, ConcreteAggregate. |
| **Benefits** | Encapsulation, flexibility (multiple iterators for one collection), uniform client code. |
| **Java support** | `java.util.Iterator<E>` and `java.lang.Iterable<E>` (used by `for‑each`). |
| **When to implement** | Custom data structures that need a stable iteration interface. |

With this understanding you can:

* Choose an appropriate iterator for any collection (`ListIterator` for bidirectional access, `Set.iterator()` for unique elements, etc.).  
* Create your own collection and iterator if you need special traversal logic (e.g., tree in‑order, depth‑first, lazy loading).  

Happy iterating!
